  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "jmgIdPh1d9Sz4xe72PFp19YSfXI=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:32:23.769Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The mechanism is a Maarch Courrier or Alfresco hook dispatching a typed operation on mail-flow approval. Mail flow is the useful specialisation here: incoming correspondence in a French public body is routed, assigned and signed off, and the approval is the event the process exists to produce.\n\nThe actionable content is ownership. AmeXio Group owns Atol CD and also XeniT, which carries the seven-lead Alfresco demo, so two entries that look like independent leads are one account. The recorded instruction is to coordinate as one group conversation. Approaching them separately risks two uncoordinated pitches into the same organisation, with the second one arriving as a surprise.\n\nThe single lead recorded here is Atol CD, based in Dijon with five further sites, marked \"2 \u00b7 next\"; XeniT sits in the Alfresco cohort at \"2 \u00b7 next\" as well. Their shared Alfresco surface means one integration plausibly serves both, which strengthens the case for a single conversation rather than merely tidying it.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- Atol CD (FR Dijon +5) \u2014 not published \u00b7 contact@atolcd.com \u00b7 https://atolcd.com\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Maarch Courrier / Alfresco hook dispatching a typed operation on mail-flow approval. NOTE: AmeXio Group owns them and XeniT \u2014 coordinate as one group conversation.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:32:23.769637Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:32:23.769641Z"
    }
  },
